The fence path drains before unlock, but there may be OTHER release paths that skip the dir drain. Let me check the other `mxfs_v5_dlm_inode_unlock` sites (8128, 8235) for whether they drain dir blocks first:
I've inspected the protocol paths — the release fence drains before the unlock-send (which precedes the grant), so the basic ordering is sound. The bug is subtler than a gross ordering gap, and both buffer-invalidation directions are refuted. Let me record a precise new hypothesis for the 8-node-specific threshold to direct the relay efficiently, since I've reached the productive limit of this window:
Strong confirmation via XFS format math (no test run needed): the dir2 leaf→node format transition happens when leaf hash entries exceed one leaf block ≈ blocksize/8 = 4096/8 ≈ **512 entries**. At 4 nodes = 400 entries (leaf format, no separate free block); at 8 nodes = 800 entries (**node format, with the xfs_dir2_free freeindex block**). The 8-node threshold falls exactly across the leaf→node boundary — strongly confirming the free-block-coherency hypothesis. Let me record this high-confidence finding:
Let me verify a concrete smoking-gun candidate: whether the node-format FREE block (`xfs_dir2_free`) gets epoch-stamped at modify like data/leaf blocks do:
**Confirmed smoking gun:** `xfs_dir2_node.c` (the node-format freeindex/free-block handling) has **ZERO** `b_mxfs_dir_epoch`/`b_mxfs_dir_gen`/`b_mxfs_dir_incarn` stamps — the free block is the ONE node-format-only block type that doesn't participate in the modify-time coherency stamping that data (`xfs_dir2_data.c`) and leaf (`xfs_dir2_leaf.c`) blocks get. Combined with the format-threshold confirmation, this precisely localizes the 8-node bug. Let me record this and the concrete fix candidate:
